    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>’s North American empire, reaching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#st-lawrence-river">St Lawrence</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>.</li><li>It rested on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fur-trade">fur trade</a> and Native alliances, but had only about 70,000 settlers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#canada">Canada</a> by 1760.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a> conquered it in the Seven Years War, yet its language, law and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#catholicism">Catholicism</a> survive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quebec">Quebec</a> today.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Between 1663 and 1673 about 800 young women, the filles du roi, were sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> at royal expense to marry settlers, and millions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#french-canadians">French Canadians</a> descend from them.</p></aside>
    </section>

