    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#jacques-cartier"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacques-cartier.jpg" alt="Jacques Cartier" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#jacques-cartier">Jacques Cartier</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques_Cartier_1851-1852.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Théophile Hamel / After François Nicholas Riss, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>’s claim in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#north-america">North America</a> began in 1534, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jacques-cartier">Jacques Cartier</a>, sailing for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francis-i-duke-of-nevers">Francis I</a>, explored the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#st-lawrence-river">St Lawrence</a> and planted a cross on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gaspe-peninsula">Gaspé Peninsula</a>. On his second voyage in 1535–1536 he went up the St Lawrence to the Iroquoian villages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#stadacona">Stadacona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hochelaga">Hochelaga</a>, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quebec">Quebec City</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shobak-castle">Montreal</a>, but an attempt at settlement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jean-francois-de-la-rocque-de-roberval">Sieur de Roberval</a> in 1541–1543 failed. For the rest of the sixteenth century French contact was limited to fishermen and fur traders. Permanent colonisation began in the early seventeenth century. After a first settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#port-royal-acadia">Port-Royal</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#acadia">Acadia</a> in 1605, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a> founded Quebec in 1608 as a fur-trading post, allied himself with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#innu">Montagnais</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algonquin-people">Algonquin</a> and Huron, and explored as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-huron">Lake Huron</a>. Growth was slow. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#company-of-one-hundred-associates">Company of One Hundred Associates</a>, created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a> in 1627, was meant to bring settlers, but English privateers under the Kirke brothers captured Quebec in 1629, and it was only restored to France in 1632. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#trois-rivieres">Trois-Rivières</a> was founded in 1634 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#montreal">Ville-Marie</a>, later Montreal, in 1642 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paul-de-chomedey-de-maisonneuve">Paul de Chomedey de Maisonneuve</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jeanne-mance">Jeanne Mance</a> as a religious mission. By 1663, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> took the colony under direct royal government, it still had only about 3,000 French inhabitants.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#st-lawrence-river"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river.jpg" alt="St. Lawrence River" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence River</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_fleuve_Saint-Laurent.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">abdallahh, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>New France rested on two economies. Along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#st-lawrence-river">St Lawrence</a>, settlers known as <em>habitants</em> farmed long narrow strips of land running back from the river, held from <em>seigneurs</em> under the seigneurial system, and grew wheat, raised livestock and paid modest dues. The interior economy was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fur-trade">fur trade</a>, above all in beaver pelts for the European hat industry, which depended on alliances with Native peoples who trapped and transported furs in exchange for cloth, kettles, tools, guns and alcohol. Young men went west as <em>coureurs des bois</em> and, later, licensed <em>voyageurs</em>, paddling canoes to posts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>. Immigration from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> remained small: about 800 young women, the <em>filles du roi</em>, were sent between 1663 and 1673 to marry settlers, and most later growth came from a high birth rate, so the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#canada">Canada</a> reached only about 70,000 by 1760, compared with more than a million in the British colonies. Fisheries on the Atlantic coast, shipbuilding and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abbey-of-saint-maurice-dagaune">Saint-Maurice</a> ironworks added to the economy, but the colony relied heavily on the Crown. The Catholic Church shaped daily life. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionaries, whose <em>Relations</em> reported on Native peoples to readers in France, worked among the Huron, the Ursuline <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marie-of-the-incarnation-ursuline">Marie de l’Incarnation</a> ran a school for girls at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quebec">Quebec</a> from 1639, and parishes, hospitals and seminaries organised society.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-monongahela"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-monongahela.jpg" alt="Battle of the Monongahela" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-monongahela">Battle of the Monongahela</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Washington_the_soldier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques Auguste Regnier, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>New France was almost always outnumbered by its English rivals and depended on Native alliances, a militia that included all able-bodied men, and from the 1680s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#troupes-de-la-marine">Troupes de la Marine</a>, regular soldiers under the navy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#samuel-de-champlain">Champlain</a>’s fight against the Mohawk near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-champlain">Lake Champlain</a> in 1609 began a long hostility with the Iroquois Confederacy, whose raids in the 1650s threatened the colony’s survival. In 1665 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> sent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#carignan-salieres-regiment">Carignan-Salières Regiment</a>, some 1,200 men, which burned Mohawk villages in 1666 and forced a peace. Wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a> followed. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-de-buade-de-frontenac">Frontenac</a> repulsed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#william-phips">Sir William Phips</a>’s attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quebec">Quebec</a> in 1690, and French and Native raiding parties struck English frontier settlements such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#schenectady-new-york">Schenectady</a> in 1690 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#deerfield-massachusetts">Deerfield</a> in 1704. The fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louisbourg">Louisbourg</a> on Île Royale, begun around 1720, guarded the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#st-lawrence-river">St Lawrence</a> but fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#new-england">New England</a> forces in 1745, before being returned in 1748. In the Seven Years War, French and Native forces defeated General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#edward-braddock">Edward Braddock</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-monongahela">Monongahela</a> in 1755, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-joseph-de-montcalm">Marquis de Montcalm</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fort-oswego">Fort Oswego</a> in 1756 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fort-william-henry">Fort William Henry</a> in 1757 and won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-carillon">Carillon</a> in 1758. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a>’s naval power and far larger armies then prevailed. Louisbourg fell in 1758, and Quebec in September 1759 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-plains-of-abraham">Battle of the Plains of Abraham</a>, where both Montcalm and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#james-wolfe">James Wolfe</a> were killed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#custom-of-paris"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/custom-of-paris.jpg" alt="Custom of Paris" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#custom-of-paris">Custom of Paris</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Custom_of_Paris_as_Practised_in_Quebec_during_the_French_government.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Guy Carleton (died 1808), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From 1663 New France was a royal province administered on the model of a French province. The governor general, a noble soldier, commanded the military and handled diplomacy with Native nations and rival colonies. The intendant directed justice, police and finance, and the first great intendant, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jean-talon">Jean Talon</a>, who served in 1665–1668 and 1670–1672, promoted immigration, agriculture, shipbuilding and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#census">census</a>, the first taken in New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> in 1666. A Sovereign Council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quebec">Quebec</a>, later called the Superior Council, registered laws and served as the highest court, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#custom-of-paris">Custom of Paris</a> was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#civil-law">civil law</a> of the colony. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">bishop</a> of Quebec, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francois-de-laval">François de Laval</a>, vicar apostolic from 1658 and bishop from 1674, headed a powerful church that also founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seminaire-de-quebec">Séminaire de Québec</a> in 1663. Policy was set at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#versailles">Versailles</a> by the Ministry of the Marine, and letters took months to cross the Atlantic. There was no elected assembly. Tensions between governors and intendants were frequent, notably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-de-buade-de-frontenac">Louis de Buade de Frontenac</a> in the 1670s, and the last intendant, François Bigot, was later convicted in France of corruption. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louisiana">Louisiana</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pierre-le-moyne-diberville">Pierre Le Moyne d’Iberville</a> in 1699, had its own governor and became a separate colony, while the western posts were run by military commandants who relied on gift-giving and diplomacy with their Native allies.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#detroit"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/detroit.jpg" alt="Detroit" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#detroit">Detroit</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detroit_Skyline_from_Windsor_2025-09-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TheWxResearcher, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>New France shaped the map and peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#north-america">North America</a> far beyond its small population. French explorers charted much of the interior: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-jolliet">Louis Jolliet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jacques-marquette">Jacques Marquette</a> descended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> in 1673, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rene-robert-cavelier-sieur-de-la-salle">René-Robert Cavelier de La Salle</a> reached its mouth in 1682 and claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louisiana">Louisiana</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pierre-gaultier-de-varennes-sieur-de-la-verendrye">La Vérendrye</a> family pushed west towards the prairies in the 1730s and 1740s. Posts and missions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#detroit">Detroit</a>, founded in 1701, Mobile and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, founded in 1718, became lasting cities. French alliances with Native nations created a distinctive world of trade, intermarriage and diplomacy around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>, which the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#richard-white-historian">Richard White</a> called the middle ground. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-peace-of-montreal">Great Peace of Montreal</a> of 1701, signed by about forty Native nations, ended a century of war with the Iroquois. The colony’s most enduring legacy is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#french-canadians">French Canada</a>. Its language, Catholic faith, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#civil-law">civil law</a> and seigneurial landholding survived the British conquest and were guaranteed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quebec-act">Quebec Act</a> of 1774. Today some seven million French-speaking Quebecers and francophone communities across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#canada">Canada</a> trace their origins largely to the roughly 10,000 French immigrants who settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#st-lawrence-river">St Lawrence</a> valley. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#metis">Métis</a> of western Canada and the French heritage of Louisiana, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cajuns">Cajuns</a> descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#acadians">Acadians</a> exiled after 1755, are further legacies.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-utrecht"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht.jpg" alt="Treaty of Utrecht" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Treaty_of_Utrecht_(clean).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The_Treaty_of_Utrecht.jpg: Original uploader was RedCoat10 at en.wikipedia derivative work: Angel paez (talk), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>New France’s weakness lay in its population and its dependence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>. While the British colonies grew to well over a million people by 1760, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#canada">Canada</a> had about 70,000, and its long line of posts from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#st-lawrence-river">St Lawrence</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ohio">Ohio</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> was thinly held. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> of 1713 had already surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#acadia">Acadia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hudson-bay">Hudson Bay</a> region to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a>. Conflict over the Ohio country, where the French built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fort-duquesne">Fort Duquesne</a> in 1754, opened the final war. After early victories, the colony faced British naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#naval-blockade">blockade</a>, poor harvests, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inflation">inflation</a> and a British government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#william-pitt-the-elder">William Pitt</a> determined to conquer it. In 1758 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louisbourg">Louisbourg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fort-frontenac">Fort Frontenac</a> and Fort Duquesne fell, and in 1759 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fort-niagara">Fort Niagara</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quebec">Quebec</a> were taken. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francois-gaston-de-levis">Chevalier de Lévis</a> defeated the British at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#church-of-sainte-foy-selestat">Sainte-Foy</a> in April 1760 but could not retake Quebec once British ships arrived in the spring, and on 8 September 1760 Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pierre-de-rigaud-marquis-de-vaudreuil-cavagnial">Pierre de Rigaud de Vaudreuil</a> surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shobak-castle">Montreal</a> and the whole of Canada. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 10 February 1763 France ceded Canada and the lands east of the Mississippi to Britain, having already given western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louisiana">Louisiana</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> in 1762, and kept only the fishing islands of Saint-Pierre and Miquelon. Canada became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#province-of-quebec-1763-1791">British Province of Quebec</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>New France was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>’s North American empire, which from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quebec">Quebec</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shobak-castle">Montreal</a> reached through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louisiana">Louisiana</a> by means of fur-trade alliances with Native nations; thinly settled and conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a> in the Seven Years War, it left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#french-canadians">French Canada</a> as its lasting legacy.</em></p>

